<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<chapter xml:id="course-16">
  <title>Homological algebra</title>
  <section xml:id="course-16-lesson-01">
    <title>Exact sequences</title>
    <p>Exactness formalizes the idea that every obstruction to one map is accounted for by the preceding map.</p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>A sequence <m>A\to</m> <m>B\to</m> <m>C</m> is exact at <m>B</m> if <m>\operatorname{im}</m> <m>f=\operatorname{ker}</m> <m>g</m>, where <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are the successive maps. In particular <m>gf=0</m>, but this condition alone is weaker than exactness.</p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <proposition>
      <statement>
        <p>A sequence <m>0\to</m> <m>A</m> <m>\to</m> <m>B</m> <m>\to</m> <m>C</m> <m>\to 0</m> is exact precisely when the first nonzero map is injective, the second is surjective, and the image of the first equals the kernel of the second.</p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>At <m>A</m>, the incoming image is zero, so the outgoing kernel is zero. At <m>C</m> the outgoing kernel is all of <m>C</m>, so the incoming image is all of <m>C</m>. The middle equality is exactly the definition.</p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
    <example>
      <p>For <m>n\ge 2</m>, the sequence <m>0\to \mathbb Z</m> <m>\to \mathbb Z</m> <m>\to \mathbb Z /n\mathbb Z \to 0</m> is exact with maps multiplication by <m>n</m> and reduction modulo <m>n</m>. It does not split: any map from the finite group <m>\mathbb Z /n\mathbb Z</m> to the torsion-free group <m>\mathbb Z</m> is zero.</p>
    </example>
    <theorem>
      <title>quotient interpretation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>For a short exact sequence of modules, <m>C</m> is canonically isomorphic to <m>B/\operatorname{im}</m> <m>f</m> by the first isomorphism theorem. This does not identify <m>B</m> with <m>A\oplus C</m> unless the sequence splits.</p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>
    <exercises>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>Give maps with <m>gf=0</m> but which are not exact at <m>B</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>Take <m>A=B=C=\mathbb Z</m> and both maps zero. The first image is zero but the second kernel is <m>\mathbb Z</m>.</p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </exercises>
    <p>Related concepts: Modules, Homology, Tor, Ext.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="course-16-lesson-02">
    <title>Split exact sequences</title>
    <p>A split extension has no hidden interaction between its submodule and quotient.</p>
    <theorem>
      <title>splitting lemma</title>
      <statement>
        <p>For a short exact sequence <m>0\to A</m> —<m>i\to</m> <m>B</m> —<m>p\to</m> <m>C\to 0</m> of modules, the following are equivalent: <m>i</m> has a left inverse, <m>p</m> has a right inverse, and the sequence is isomorphic to the standard direct-sum sequence.</p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>Given a section <m>s:C\to B</m> with <m>ps=id</m>, define <m>F:A\oplus C\to B</m> by <m>F(a,c)=i(a)+s(c)</m>. Applying <m>p</m> proves injectivity; subtracting <m>s(p(b))</m> from <m>b</m> proves surjectivity. The inverse composed with projection onto <m>A</m> gives a retraction. Conversely if <m>r</m> <m>i=id</m>, then <m>\operatorname{ker}</m> <m>r</m> maps isomorphically onto <m>C</m> under <m>p</m>: injectivity follows from exactness, and <m>b-i(r(b))</m> gives a lift in <m>\operatorname{ker}</m> <m>r</m>. Invert that restriction to obtain <m>s</m>.</p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>
    <example>
      <p>Every short exact sequence of vector spaces splits by extending a basis of the subspace, assuming the usual choice principle. The splitting is usually not canonical.</p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <title>Counterexample</title>
      <p>The sequence <m>0\to \mathbb Z</m> —<m>2\to \mathbb Z \to \mathbb Z /2\mathbb Z \to 0</m> does not split, since a section would send a nonzero torsion element into a torsion-free group.</p>
    </example>
    <exercises>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>If <m>C</m> is free, why does the sequence split?</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>Choose a lift in <m>B</m> for each basis element of <m>C</m> and extend linearly to a section.</p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </exercises>
  </section>
